      "Internal conflict forced the Sayfawa dynasty to relocate west of Lake Chad in the late 14th century, founding the Bornu Empire — together, Kanem-Bornu became one of the longest-lived states in African history, lasting in various forms into the 19th century and controlling trans-Saharan trade in slaves, ivory, ostrich feathers, and natron (a mineral salt harvested from Lake Chad).",
